"""Concise rhythm-mechanism teaching for retained Chapter 14 questions."""
from cardiology_14_compact_conduction import B,S
edits={
'06-001':S('Physiological Sinus Acceleration',[B('**Matching sinus P morphology with gradual onset/offset favors sinus tachycardia.**','Fever and volume depletion provide plausible drivers; rate alone cannot identify the mechanism.')],
 'Treat the Trigger',[B('**Correct dehydration, fever, hypoxemia, pain or other causes and reassess.**','Tachycardia may preserve output; indiscriminate AV nodal blockade can worsen perfusion.'),B('**Abrupt fixed-rate episodes or different atrial activation suggest another rhythm.**','Persistent tachycardia after correction warrants further evaluation, rather than assuming every rapid sinus rhythm is benign.')]),
'06-002':S('Typical AVNRT Pattern',[B('**Very short RP with abrupt regular narrow tachycardia favors slow–fast AVNRT.**','Near-simultaneous atrial/ventricular activation may produce pseudo-r-prime in V1 or pseudo-S waves inferiorly.')],
 'Surface ECG Limits',[B('**Orthodromic AVRT and other mechanisms remain possible.**','No delta wave does not exclude a concealed retrograde accessory pathway.'),B('**Capture a 12-lead during tachycardia and compare baseline.**','Use onset, termination and atrial timing; EPS defines mechanism when needed. Instability dictates immediate treatment irrespective of the suspected circuit.')]),
'06-003':S('Long-RP Differential',[B('**RP longer than PR describes timing, not a unique mechanism.**','Consider atypical AVNRT, slowly conducting orthodromic AVRT such as PJRT, and focal atrial tachycardia.')],
 'Interpret Adenosine Carefully',[B('**Termination does not prove one circuit.**','AV-node-dependent reentry may stop, but some focal atrial tachycardias are also adenosine-sensitive.'),B('**Inspect atrial activity during AV block and termination.**','Negative inferior P waves suggest activation direction, not definitive circuit anatomy; recurrent or unclear episodes may require EPS.')]),
'06-004':S('Continuing Atrial Activity',[B('**Transient AV block exposes an atrial rhythm that continues at its original cycle length.**','Discrete abnormal P waves separated by an isoelectric baseline support atrial tachycardia.')],
 'What the Response Establishes',[B('**Ordinary orthodromic AVRT requires AV nodal antegrade conduction**, so blocking that limb usually terminates the circuit.'),B('**Adenosine slowing is not synonymous with conversion.**','Record the atria throughout the maneuver; AVNRT can occasionally continue despite block below its circuit, so use the full atrial pattern rather than AV block alone.')]),
'06-005':S('Focal Atrial Tachycardia',[B('**A consistent non-sinus P morphology with discrete atrial waves supports a focal source.**','Warm-up/cool-down suggests abnormal automaticity; some focal AT uses triggered activity or localized reentry.')],
 'Assess Consequences',[B('**Persistence through AV block supports an atrial driver.**','The ventricular response can vary without changing the atrial cycle.'),B('**Incessant AT can cause tachycardia-mediated cardiomyopathy.**','Assess burden, LV function and reversible triggers; recurrent symptomatic or incessant disease merits specialist treatment, often including ablation assessment.')]),
'06-006':S('MAT Criteria',[B('**Irregular atrial rhythm >100/min with ≥3 P-wave morphologies in one lead.**','PP, PR and RR vary; an isoelectric baseline separates P waves. A slower counterpart is wandering atrial pacemaker.')],
 'Management',[B('**Treat pulmonary/systemic illness and correct potassium/magnesium.**','Hypoxemia, severe COPD, adrenergic stress and contributing drugs are common contexts.'),B('**Do not label it AF merely because RR is irregular.**','Discrete varying P waves distinguish MAT; cardioversion is usually ineffective, and anticoagulation is not prescribed solely for MAT.')]),
'06-007':S('Flutter With 2:1 Conduction',[B('**Atrial activity near 300/min can produce a ventricular rate near 150/min.**','Alternate flutter waves may be hidden in QRS/T waves; 150/min is a clue, not a diagnostic threshold.')],
 'Unmask the Atria',[B('**Transient AV nodal slowing reveals continuing macroreentry.**','Typical counterclockwise CTI-dependent flutter often has negative inferior waves and positive V1 activity.'),B('**Ventricular slowing is not necessarily termination.**','Document the atrial rhythm; assess stability, stroke prevention and rhythm strategy according to duration and clinical risk.')]),
'06-008':S('Junctional Automaticity',[B('**Gradual narrow-complex acceleration with slower independent sinus P waves supports focal junctional tachycardia.**','The AV junction activates the ventricles while atrial and ventricular timing can dissociate.')],
 'Postoperative Context',[B('**Review catecholamines, stress, ischemia, electrolytes and drugs.**','Digoxin toxicity and postoperative tissue irritation are relevant alternative settings.'),B('**Assess perfusion and ventricular function.**','Nonreentrant impulse formation differs from abrupt AVNRT; treatment targets triggers and rate/physiology, with specialist input when persistent or unstable.')]),
'06-017':S('Direction of the AVRT Circuit',[B('**Orthodromic:** AV node/His–Purkinje antegradely, accessory pathway retrogradely.','QRS is usually narrow unless aberrancy or another conduction abnormality exists.')],
 'Related Mechanisms',[B('**Antidromic:** accessory pathway antegradely, with return through the AV node or another pathway.','Ventricular activation is pre-excited and generally wide.'),B('**A pathway may be a bystander during AF, flutter or AVNRT.**','Manifest pre-excitation does not prove that every recorded tachycardia uses AV reentry; EPS establishes circuit participation when necessary.')]),
'06-018':S('Retrograde-Only Pathway',[B('**A concealed accessory pathway lacks antegrade conduction**, so no sinus delta wave is expected.','It can still return activation from ventricle to atrium during orthodromic AVRT.')],
 'Risk and Treatment',[B('**A genuinely retrograde-only pathway does not transmit AF rapidly antegradely.**','This distinction concerns pathway physiology, not a declaration that all symptoms are harmless.'),B('**Document recurrent tachycardia and assess symptom burden.**','EPS and ablation can establish and treat a symptomatic pathway; a normal resting PR/QRS does not exclude it.')]),
'06-019':S('Pre-Excited AF',[B('**Very rapid irregular rhythm with changing QRS width/morphology** suggests AF conducted through an accessory pathway.','Variable fusion of AV nodal and accessory conduction explains beat-to-beat changes.')],
 'Avoid AV Nodal Blockade',[B('**AV nodal blockers can accelerate pathway-mediated ventricular activation and trigger VF.**','Avoid adenosine, beta-blockers, verapamil/diltiazem, digoxin and IV amiodarone in this setting.'),B('**Unstable patients require electrical cardioversion.**','Stable cases may receive specialist-guided IV procainamide or ibutilide; consider definitive pathway ablation after stabilization.')]),
'06-021':S('Accessory-Pathway Risk',[B('**SPERRI ≤250 ms during AF is high risk under ESC 2019.**','SPERRI 220 ms demonstrates rapid antegrade pathway conduction; it is not an AV nodal refractory-period measurement.')],
 'Broader EPS Assessment',[B('**Short pathway antegrade refractory period, multiple pathways and inducible AVRT** contribute to risk assessment.'),B('**Catecholamines can expose faster conduction.**','Intermittent loss of pre-excitation can suggest lower risk but is not an absolute guarantee.'),B('**Discuss ablation according to risk, occupation and patient preferences**, balancing pathway location and procedural hazards.')]),
'06-027':S('Postablation Macroreentry',[B('**Scar and lesion gaps can support non-CTI-dependent atrial reentry.**','Perimitral and roof-dependent circuits are examples; surface flutter morphology is unreliable after prior ablation.')],
 'Map Before Ablating',[B('**Activation mapping plus appropriate entrainment identifies circuit participation and a critical isthmus.**','Account for the whole cycle and distinguish active circuit from passive activation.'),B('**Intact CTI block does not exclude left atrial flutter.**','Empirical repetition of CTI ablation is poorly targeted when the clinical circuit lies elsewhere; assess lesion gaps and procedural risks.')]),
'06-028':S('Confirm an Optical Alert',[B('**Photoplethysmography detects pulse irregularity, not diagnostic atrial electrical activity.**','Obtain an interpretable ECG and clinician review before establishing clinical AF.')],
 'Recording Standards',[B('**ESC 2024 accepts conventional 12-lead or interpretable single-/multilead ECG documentation.**','A usual ten-second 12-lead recording need not be extended to thirty seconds to count as that conventional recording.'),B('**Longer ambulatory episodes require contextual interpretation.**','Duration, signal quality and clinical consequences guide further monitoring; an optical alert alone is not an anticoagulation mandate.')]),
'07-002':S('VT-Supporting Signs',[B('**AV dissociation plus a capture beat strongly supports VT.**','A sinus impulse occasionally reaches the ventricle when excitable, producing a near-normal narrow QRS within the wide tachycardia.')],
 'Capture, Fusion and Limits',[B('**Fusion combines sinus and ventricular activation**, producing an intermediate QRS shape.'),B('**Their absence does not exclude VT.**','P waves can be concealed, and VT may have 1:1 retrograde conduction.'),B('**Treat uncertain regular wide tachycardia as VT**, especially with structural disease; instability requires immediate synchronized cardioversion when feasible.')]),
'07-003':S('Wide-Complex Morphology',[B('**No RS complex in any precordial lead is a Brugada-algorithm VT criterion.**','If RS exists, a longest RS interval >100 ms also supports VT; other steps assess AV dissociation and morphology.')],
 'Clinical Interpretation',[B('**Prior MI markedly increases the likelihood of scar VT.**','Algorithms are probabilistic and have limitations with drugs, pre-excitation, metabolic disease and unusual substrates.'),B('**Do not use a negative morphological rule to declare SVT certain.**','Compare baseline ECG, assess stability and avoid hazardous empirical AV nodal treatment in undifferentiated wide tachycardia.')]),
'07-004':S('Two Polymorphic Mechanisms',[B('**Torsades is polymorphic VT with prolonged QT in the intervening rhythm.**','Pause-dependent onset after a QT-prolonging drug supports acquired torsades; normal-QT polymorphic VT during chest pain raises ischemic concern.')],
 'Mechanism-Specific Treatment',[B('**Sustained polymorphic VT needs an unsynchronized shock**, because reliable synchronization is difficult.'),B('**For acquired torsades:** stop culprit drugs, correct electrolytes and give IV magnesium; recurrent pause-dependent episodes may need rate acceleration.'),B('**For normal-QT ischemic polymorphic VT:** urgently treat ischemia.','A twisting appearance alone does not justify diagnosing long-QT torsades.')]),
'07-005':S('Bidirectional VT',[B('**Alternating frontal QRS axis on successive beats** defines the pattern.','Digoxin toxicity, CPVT and Andersen–Tawil syndrome are important associations; the pattern is not synonymous with torsades.')],
 'Use the Clinical Context',[B('**Digoxin exposure, vomiting and new renal dysfunction favor toxicity here.**','CPVT typically appears with adrenergic stress in a younger structurally normal heart.'),B('**Stop digoxin and assess perfusion, renal function and electrolytes urgently.**','Life-threatening digoxin-related ventricular arrhythmia warrants digoxin immune Fab and specialist support; do not rely on an isolated level to delay treatment.')]),
'07-026':S('Scar-Reentry Substrate',[B('**Surviving myocardial channels within/along scar permit slow conduction and reentry.**','Postinfarction monomorphic VT is not simply electrical activity arising from uniformly dead tissue.')],
 'Mapping When VT Is Intolerable',[B('**Substrate mapping in sinus or paced rhythm identifies abnormal potentials and conduction channels.**','Activation/entrainment mapping is useful when VT is tolerated; prolonged induced hypotension is not required to locate a target.'),B('**Ablation strategy remains individualized.**','Integrate imaging, electrograms and induced rhythm information, with hemodynamic support and recurrence prevention when appropriate.')]),
'07-029':S('Idiopathic RVOT VT',[B('**LBBB-like morphology with inferior axis** commonly reflects activation from a superior right-ventricular source.','Many cases involve cAMP-dependent calcium loading and delayed-afterdepolarization triggered activity.')],
 'Interpret Adenosine Response',[B('**Adenosine can terminate selected outflow VTs.**','Response does not prove that a tachycardia is supraventricular; rate and drug response are not sufficient classifiers.'),B('**Exclude structural/inherited disease before calling it idiopathic.**','ARVC and other RV substrates can mimic the surface pattern; assess ECG, imaging, family history and symptoms. Recurrent symptomatic disease may benefit from ablation.')]),
'07-031':S('Posterior Fascicular VT',[B('**RBBB-like QRS with superior axis** is typical of posterior fascicular reentry.','Specialized Purkinje tissue participates, so QRS may be relatively narrow for VT.')],
 'Verapamil Requires a Known Mechanism',[B('**IV verapamil can terminate established, tolerated verapamil-sensitive fascicular VT.**','Monitor pressure and conduction; this is not an empirical diagnostic trial for an uncertain wide rhythm.'),B('**Unstable tachycardia needs electrical treatment.**','Recurrent fascicular VT can be treated by catheter ablation; reconsider structural disease if the phenotype or imaging is atypical.')]),
'07-039':S('Type 1 Brugada Pattern',[B('**Coved ST elevation ≥2 mm followed by negative T in V1 or V2** is the diagnostic type 1 pattern.','Standard fourth or deliberately recorded second/third intercostal positions are valid; document placement.')],
 'Syndrome and Risk',[B('**A spontaneous type 1 pattern without another explanation supports Brugada syndrome.**','Exclude ischemic, structural and metabolic phenocopies; drug-induced findings require additional clinical assessment.'),B('**Treat fever and avoid provoking drugs**, with inherited-arrhythmia evaluation.'),B('**ICD decisions depend on arrest, documented arrhythmia and syncope risk**, not the ECG label alone in every asymptomatic patient.')]),
'07-044':S('Early Repolarization Pattern',[B('**Terminal QRS notching/slurring with J elevation ≥1 mm in ≥2 adjacent inferior/lateral leads** describes the pattern.','It is common in healthy people; inferior high-amplitude/horizontal-ST phenotypes may carry greater population risk.')],
 'Pattern Is Not Syndrome',[B('**Early-repolarization syndrome concerns otherwise unexplained polymorphic VT/VF in a patient with the pattern.**','The supplied asymptomatic adult has no such event, so the ECG alone does not establish that syndrome.'),B('**Use symptoms and family history to guide assessment.**','Do not label new ischemic chest pain benign merely because an earlier tracing had early repolarization.')]),
'08-028':S('Named 2010 Repolarization Criterion',[B('**T inversion in V1–V3 or beyond in age >14 without complete RBBB is a major 2010 ARVC criterion.**','The stem explicitly uses that framework; T inversion is a repolarization finding, not an epsilon wave.')],
 'Avoid Single-Sign Diagnosis',[B('**ARVC diagnosis combines structural, tissue, rhythm, ECG and familial/genetic evidence.**','Other diseases, athlete patterns and technical errors must be considered.'),B('**Complete RBBB changes criterion interpretation.**','Confirm lead placement and reproducibility; an isolated major ECG criterion does not alone establish definite ARVC or an ICD indication.')]),
}
